### What is an anagram?

Two strings are anagrams if one is a rearrangement of the other: they contain exactly the same characters with the same frequencies. For example, `"listen"` and `"silent"` are anagrams. `"hello"` and `"world"` are not.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AnagramBasics {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("listen", "silent")); // true
        System.out.println(isAnagram("hello", "world"));   // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### Why check lengths first?

If two strings have different lengths, they cannot possibly be anagrams. Checking lengths first is a cheap O(1) operation that rejects obvious non-anagrams immediately without doing any character counting.

The length check also establishes an invariant: if both strings have the same length and no count goes negative during the decrement pass, then all counts must end at zero. This means the single-array trick is sufficient without a final verification loop.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LengthCheckFirst {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) {
            System.out.println("Rejected: different lengths");
            return false;
        }
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("abc", "abcd"));
        System.out.println(isAnagram("abc", "cba"));
    }
}
```

```text
Rejected: different lengths
false
true
```

### How does the frequency-count method work?

The frequency-count method uses an `int[26]` array where each index represents a letter. Loop through the first string and increment the count for each character. Then loop through the second string and decrement. If any count goes negative, the second string has more of that character than the first, so they are not anagrams.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FrequencyCountAnagram {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String s1, String s2) {
        if (s1.length() != s2.length()) return false;

        int[] freq = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < s1.length(); i++) {
            freq[s1.charAt(i) - 'a']++;
            freq[s2.charAt(i) - 'a']--;
        }
        for (int count : freq) {
            if (count != 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String payee1 = "rajesh";
        String payee2 = "jeshar";
        System.out.println(payee1 + " and " + payee2 + ": " + isAnagram(payee1, payee2));
    }
}
```

```text
rajesh and jeshar: true
```

### How is the frequency-count method different from sort-and-compare?

The sort-and-compare method converts both strings to character arrays, sorts them, and checks if the sorted arrays are equal. It is simpler to write but slower.

| Feature | Frequency count | Sort-and-compare |
|---|---|---|
| Time complexity | O(n) | O(n log n) |
| Space complexity | O(1) for 26-letter alphabet | O(n) for sorted copies |
| Early exit | Yes, on first negative count | No, must sort completely |
| Clarity | Moderate | Very simple |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class AnagramTwoMethods {
    public static boolean sortCompare(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        char[] ac = a.toCharArray();
        char[] bc = b.toCharArray();
        Arrays.sort(ac);
        Arrays.sort(bc);
        return Arrays.equals(ac, bc);
    }

    public static boolean freqCount(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Sort: " + sortCompare("listen", "silent"));
        System.out.println("Freq: " + freqCount("listen", "silent"));
    }
}
```

```text
Sort: true
Freq: true
```

### What about Unicode and arbitrary characters?

When the character set is not limited to lowercase ASCII, a fixed `int[26]` array is not enough. Use a `HashMap<Character, Integer>` instead. The logic is the same: increment for the first string, decrement for the second, and check that all counts end at zero.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class UnicodeAnagram {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        Map<Character, Integer> freq = new HashMap<>();
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) freq.merge(c, 1, Integer::sum);
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) freq.merge(c, -1, Integer::sum);
        return freq.values().stream().allMatch(v -> v == 0);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAnagram("listen", "silent")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
true
```

### What are the edge cases?

Edge cases include empty strings (two empty strings are anagrams), single-character strings, strings with repeated characters, and strings that differ by just one character.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AnagramEdgeCases {
    public static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + isAnagram("", ""));         // true
        System.out.println("Single same: " + isAnagram("a", "a")); // true
        System.out.println("Single diff: " + isAnagram("a", "b")); // false
        System.out.println("Repeated: " + isAnagram("aab", "aba")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Single same: true
Single diff: false
Repeated: true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to handle case sensitivity. If the problem says the comparison should be case-insensitive, you must lowercase both strings first. Another mistake is using character code sums as a hash: different letter combinations can produce the same sum, leading to false positives.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class AnagramMistakes {
    // WRONG: sum of char codes is not collision-free
    public static boolean wrongAnagram(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int sum1 = 0, sum2 = 0;
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) sum1 += c;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) sum2 += c;
        return sum1 == sum2; // can give false positive
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // "ad" and "bc" have the same char sum but are NOT anagrams
        System.out.println("Wrong: " + wrongAnagram("ad", "bc")); // true (incorrect!)
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong: true
```

### What is the banking use case?

At National Bank of India, Rajesh uses the anagram check in a fraud detection pipeline. Fraudsters sometimes re-enter an existing payee name with the letters shuffled to slip past an exact-match filter. The system normalises both names by lowercasing and removing spaces, then runs the frequency count to detect shuffled duplicates and flag them for manual review by Priya.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class FraudPayeeCheck {
    public static boolean isSuspiciousDuplicate(String existing, String incoming) {
        String norm1 = existing.toLowerCase().replaceAll("\\s+", "");
        String norm2 = incoming.toLowerCase().replaceAll("\\s+", "");
        if (norm1.equals(norm2)) return false; // exact match, not suspicious
        return isAnagram(norm1, norm2); // shuffled match is suspicious
    }

    private static boolean isAnagram(String a, String b) {
        if (a.length() != b.length()) return false;
        int[] count = new int[26];
        for (char c : a.toCharArray()) count[c - 'a']++;
        for (char c : b.toCharArray()) {
            if (--count[c - 'a'] < 0) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isSuspiciousDuplicate("Priya Sharma", "Sharm Apriya")); // true
        System.out.println(isSuspiciousDuplicate("Priya Sharma", "Priya Sharma")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```
